::STAT_U01_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A survey asks students how many minutes they studied last night. What type of data is collected?
{
=A. Numerical, because minutes measure an amount of time#Minutes can be compared, ordered, and averaged because they measure time.
~B. Categorical, because the values are labels#Minutes are measured amounts, not labels. Use what the numbers mean in context.
~C. Categorical, because each student gives one answer#One answer per student does not make data categorical.
~D. Neither, because time is not data#Time can be recorded as data when it answers a statistical question.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A class records each student's favorite movie genre. What type of data is collected?
{
~A. Numerical, because the class can count responses#Counting responses later does not change the original variable.
~B. Numerical, because every response can be listed#Listing responses does not make them quantities.
~C. Both numerical and categorical#The original variable is a category, not a measurement.
=D. Categorical, because genre is a label or group#Movie genre names groups rather than measured amounts.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A data table lists student ID numbers. How should the ID numbers be classified?
{
~A. Numerical, because the values use digits#Digits alone do not make data numerical.
~B. Numerical, because the numbers can be ordered#ID order usually does not represent a measured amount.
=C. Categorical, because the numbers are labels#Student ID numbers identify people; arithmetic with the IDs is not meaningful.
~D. Both, because every label is also a number#A value used as a label should be treated as categorical data.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display is usually appropriate for counts of students in each favorite app category?
{
=A. Bar chart#A bar chart compares counts across categories.
~B. Histogram#Histograms show numerical intervals, not category labels.
~C. Dot plot#Dot plots are usually used for numerical values.
~D. Scatterplot#Scatterplots show relationships between two numerical variables.
}

::STAT_U01_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_L01_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A display shows the number of pets owned by each student. Why is this numerical data?
{
~A. Because pets are animals#The context is pets, but the variable is the number owned.
=B. Because the values are counts that can be compared and summarized#Number of pets is a count, so comparing or finding a mean is meaningful.
~C. Because every survey answer is numerical#Not every survey answer is numerical.
~D. Because the display has a title#A title helps interpretation but does not determine data type.
}
